we have a ton of legislative news to dig  into today we'll be talking about the  equality act we will be talking about  covid relief but before talking about  any of that  massive news late last night the house  of representatives  thanks to democrats mostly this was this  was democrats who voted for this  democrats in the house have passed the  massive  voting rights and anti-corruption bill  h.r  one democratic congressman ro khanna  talked to us about this when we  interviewed him last week  and this is not the first time that hr  one  has passed in the house it's the second  time that it has passed  just like the first time it passed it's  not clear how it passes the senate and  we'll talk about that but this is an  extensive bill  800 pages all sorts of of different  things contained within it  but the big items are a national system  for automatic  voter registration this is something  i've been talking about  for a decade it's something that would  dramatically increase  participation in elections and it would  ask  right now you have to opt in if you want  to register to vote the default is if  you do nothing you're not registered you  have to opt  in the system proposed in hr1  would create an automatic voter  registration system where you have to  opt  out if you don't want to be registered  to vote and why would you do that if you  don't want to vote you just wouldn't  vote  and most people would just remain  registered  the bill would require every state to  put online  options for voter registration  correcting your voter registration  canceling or designating a party  affiliation  things that in other countries are just  obvious that you have any developed  country should have these things  the bill would require at least 15  consecutive days of early voting for  federal elections  and early voting sites would have to be  open for at least 10 hours a day this  would short-circuit and preempt many of  the  state-level voter suppression tactics  that republicans have been using for a  long time  and the bill would also ban states from  restricting someone's ability to vote by  mail  and it will also require states to  prepay postage on return envelopes for  mail-in voting so it doesn't cost people  money  to vote by mail the bill would ban voter  roll  purges and also would ban the use of  non-forwardable mail  as a way to remove voters from roles  every one of these elements goes  directly at  a technique used by the right to  suppress the vote  and very interestingly hr1 establishes  a public financing of campaigns  with small donations the federal  government would do a voluntary  six to one match for candidates for  president and congress which means  every dollar a candidate raises from  small  donors the federal government would  match six times over  and the maximum small donation that  would be matched is capped at two  hundred dollars it's not funded by  taxpayer dollars  the money comes from a 4.75 fee  on criminal and civil fines fees  penalties and settlements with banks and  corporations  that do corporate malfeasance very  interesting way to structure and finance  this  and it also contains and again we can't  possibly cover  every line but there are transparency  requirements for political advertising  in hr1  it would establish nonpartisan  redistricting commissions to end  partisan gerrymandering massive issue  again we've been talking about it for a  decade it would require  presidential and vice presidential  candidates to disclose  uh 10 years of tax returns and  we know directly what that's related to  it would stop  members of congress from using taxpayer  money to settle sexual harassment or  discrimination cases  and so many other things now beautiful  bill  has passed the house how does it pass  the senate  and the answer is it doesn't unless it's  modified  significantly but maybe something can  pass  the question is why democrats control  the senate  democrats voted for this in the house  why can't democrats  pass it the answer is there are just too  many moderates  in the democratic party in the senate  and the majority is too slim  in fact it's the slimmest possible  majority the senate is 50 50 democrats  republicans  because democrats are in the white house  kamala harris becomes the tie-breaking  vote  in the senate when there is a 50-50 tie  but  this requires 60 votes to pass the  senate so in other words  i'm unsure that if it only required  50 it would pass the senate but they  need  60 votes anyway now one idea that  democrats  are thinking about is amending the  senate filibuster rules  so that any democracy reform legislation  like h.r 1  could pass with a simple majority  instead of 60 votes  now that's great if joe manchin goes for  it and that's very much  unclear if gene shaheen goes for it  i don't know if this is a bill that  jeanne shaheen  is interested in passing so right now  the situation anyway is you need 60  votes now this is an issue  on which there is almost no overlap  between democrats and republicans  we talk on this program you know some  shows love to take these black white  positions like there's no relevant  difference between democrats and  republicans  and the truth is that on some issues  there's no relevant difference between  democrats and republicans and on other  issues  they couldn't be more different  democrats at least in the house  are passing hr1 which is a massive  expansion in voting rights it would  permanently cement  many temporary expansions on the basis  of coronavirus  that's democrats at least in the house  on the other  hand as we talked about on i think it  was monday  republicans have more than 250 bills  in 43 different states to suppress  the vote to make it more difficult to  vote so  these are diametrically opposed  priorities  on these issues between democrats and  republicans democrats doing everything  they can  to increase participation in elections  republicans doing everything they can to  decrease  participation now you can take a cynical  view and say well  both sides are doing what they think is  best for them for them democrats believe  more people voting is good for democrats  republicans believe fewer people voting  is good for republicans  they are both probably correct the  difference is  one of them is actually in line with the  principles and values that this country  is based on which is  not disenfranchising people giving  people a voice  not putting not obstructing the ability  of people to participate in the  democratic process it's hard to argue  that the country is based on the values  that are exhibited by 250 republican  bills  in 43 states to suppress the vote so  yeah sure you can take the view that  it's quite simple  and it's democrats want more people to  vote so they win republicans want fewer  people to vote so they win  one of those perspectives happens to be  in line with the foundation of this  country now  as a very practical matter in the  immediate republicans  have a particularly pressing reason to  oppose hr1  which is that they're winning the house  back in 2022  could depend on whether something like  hr1 passes or doesn't pass  and we'll talk more about that in the  future that's  hr1 let's now talk about covid stimulus  one of our sponsors is monk pack  it's tough to get excited about most  granola bars  they have a lot of carbs they don't  taste that great  they don't really satisfy your cravings  but monk pack has something totally  different monk pack  keto granola bars are sweet and  delicious  with a soft and chewy texture  only 140 calories one gram of sugar  and only two to three net carbs which  make them work  perfectly for the keto or low carb  lifestyles  they come in excellent flavors like  coconut cocoa chip  honey nut blueberry almond vanilla  that's my new favorite  i love these because you can just grab  them anytime maybe it's a quick  breakfast an afternoon snack when you're  busy  forget